/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 3
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 2
    land_position 15 50
  }
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 3
    number_of_tiles 150
    zone 3
    land_position 85 50
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  create_connect_all_lands
  {
    replace_terrain GRASS ROAD
    terrain_size GRASS 1 0
  }

/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_77a - is the guide-sourced "no terrain_size entry -> radius 1,
variance 0" default real, or does the undeclared default carry hidden
variance the doc has never measured?
Feeds: docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.5 line ~524 (Sec.15 item cited there as
"32", which COLLIDES with the already-closed detached-seed item 32 - see
the doc-update note this run's write-up carries), tools/scenario-probe
README Batch 16 (RMSTEST_70's undeclared-terrain_size DIRT causeway
fragmenting into small/single-tile patches in two of its three runs)
Paired with RMSTEST_77b, whose only difference is dropping the
"terrain_size GRASS 1 0" line entirely.
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
guide:1958/1960 give radius 1 and variance 0 as the terrain_size defaults,
and Sec.6.5 has modelled undeclared terrain_size that way since rev 3. It
has never been run against the real engine. RMSTEST_70, written for an
unrelated question (does a beach pass run after S5), noticed in passing
that its own undeclared-terrain_size DIRT causeway came out fragmented -
dozens of small/single-tile patches - in two of its three runs, which a
deterministic 1-tile disc on every path tile should not produce at all: a
straight or near-straight path painted with a constant 1-tile radius and
zero variance should read as ONE contiguous patch (or very close to it),
not many.

Per guide:1965, real variance CAN produce exactly this shape: "if variance
is larger than radius, it can reduce the radius to a negative value, in
which case no terrain will be replaced around these specific locations" -
i.e. an occasional skipped tile along the path, which is indistinguishable
from a gap in a --patches read. If the undeclared default genuinely
carries variance greater than 0, gaps like RMSTEST_70's are exactly what
the rule predicts; if it does not, something else fragmented that causeway
and the "radius 1, variance 0" default stands unexplained rather than
refuted.

METHOD
Two lands, land_position pinned on opposite sides of a Normal map so the
connecting path is short, direct and geometrically simple - nothing else
on the map to paint or replace terrain, so every ROAD tile comes from this
one command. This script (a) DECLARES terrain_size GRASS 1 0 explicitly,
stating in RMS what Sec.6.5 currently only assumes as the undeclared
default. If the assumed default is correct, this script and its twin
(77b, which omits the line) should read identically - same patch count,
same shape, same fragmentation (none, ideally).

PREDICTIONS
  ONE CONTIGUOUS ROAD PATCH (or very close to it - a handful of pieces at
    most, matching ordinary A* step noise, not the dozens RMSTEST_70 saw)
    -> radius 1, variance 0 produces a clean, mostly unbroken path, which
    is the baseline 77b needs to match for the assumed default to be
    confirmed rather than merely unrefuted.
  MULTIPLE SMALL / SINGLE-TILE PATCHES -> even an EXPLICIT variance-0
    declaration fragments, which would mean the fragmentation RMSTEST_70
    saw has nothing to do with terrain_size's variance at all (a different
    mechanism entirely, e.g. the causeway's own path not being contiguous,
    or replace_terrain interacting with something else) - and this script
    existing is what would catch that alternative explanation.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. THREE RUNS, matching 77b.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches ROAD
  Read the patch COUNT first, then each patch's area and circularity. One
  patch (or a handful of large, elongated ones) is the clean reading; many
  small patches is fragmentation. Compare directly against 77b's own read
  on the same three run numbers.
================================================================================
*/
